course: dictionary
lesson: 11695
title: Attendee: people at an event
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: An attendee is a person who goes to an event. The event might be a meeting, a conference, a class or a party. Attendee is a formal word, so you will often see it on signs, in letters and in reports. «All attendees must sign in at the desk.»
explain: Look at the clues around the word. «The hall was full, and over two hundred attendees sat in rows to hear the speaker.» The people sitting in the hall are the attendees, so the word means people who came to the event.
explain: Attendee comes from attend, which means to go to or be present at something. If you know that attend means to be there, you can work out attendee. Try the swap test: put "people who came" in place of attendees, and check the sentence still makes sense.
fact: An attendee is a person who goes to an event.
fact: Events with attendees include meetings, conferences, classes and parties.
fact: In everyday talk, people often say "guests" or "people who came" instead of attendees.
fact: «All attendees must sign in.» means everyone who comes must sign in.
fact: In maths, the number of attendees is how many people were at the event.
exercise: «Every attendee received a name badge at the door.» What is an attendee? | A room in a school building | A badge that is pinned on a door | *A person who goes to an event | A person who cleans the hall
exercise: «The number of attendees at the school play was 120.» What does the number of attendees tell us? | How old the actors were | How long the play lasted | *How many people came to the play | How much the tickets cost
exercise: «All attendees must sign in.» Which everyday word could replace attendees here? | puddles | *guests | tomatoes | pencils
test: Attend means to be present at something. Which meaning fits attendee best? | A person who writes a book for a living | A food that is served at a party | *A person who is present at an event
test: «The attendees clapped when the talk finished.» Who clapped? | *The people who came to the talk | The teachers who walked past the hall | The birds sitting in the trees
exam: «The conference had fifty attendees.» Who are the attendees? | The speakers who gave the talks | The tickets sold at the door | *The people who came to the conference | The room where the talks were held
